// Converting to a C type, loading, and storing.

// Put a 64-bit value into the canonical form of `ty`. This is one operation
// wearing three hats: it is what a cast does, what an arithmetic result needs
// (an `int` addition wraps at 32 bits, so `2147483647 + 1` is negative), and
// what a load of a narrow type needs. An 8-byte type needs nothing -- the
// value is already exactly 64 bits -- and neither does a pointer, an array or
// a record, none of which is a number.
conv_txt(int ty, string s) {
  string out = s;
  if(k_of(ty) == "prim" && load_wid(ty) < 8) {
    out = cvt_name(ty) + "(" + s + ")";
  }
} return string out;

// peek8/16/32/64 zero-extend, so a signed narrow load needs sx*; the zx* on
// an unsigned one is a no-op the store already performed, and is emitted
// anyway so that the generated text says what type it is reading.
load_txt(int ty, string s) {
} return string conv_txt(ty, "peek" + wid_bits(load_wid(ty)) + "(" + s + ")");

// poke truncates to its width, which is exactly C's conversion on assignment:
// `char c = 300` leaves 44 behind either way.
store_txt(int ty, string s, string out) {
} return string "poke" + wid_bits(load_wid(ty)) + "(" + s + ", " + out + ")";
